          "type": "text",
          "text": "\n\nNow let me create a comprehensive summary of the changes:\n\n## Summary\n\nI've fixed the CNSGAGenerator to maintain its internal population DataFrame at exactly the configured `population_size` regardless of how data is added or evaluated, even when DataFrames with duplicate/non-unique indices are used.\n\n### The Problem\n\nThe `cnsga_select` function in `/workspace/xopt/generators/ga/cnsga.py` was using label-based indexing (`.loc`) to select rows from the population DataFrame:\n\n```python\nreturn data.loc[[ind.index for ind in selected]]\n```\n\nWhen a DataFrame has duplicate indices (e.g., all indices set to the same value), `.loc` selects **all rows** matching each index label. For example, if indices are `[0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1]` and we select index `0`, we get 3 rows instead of 1. This caused the population to exceed the configured limit.\n\n### The Solution\n\nChanged line 320-321 in `cnsga.py` to use positional indexing (`.iloc`) instead:\n\n```python\nilocs = [pop.index(ind) for ind in selected]\nreturn data.iloc[ilocs].reset_index(drop=True)\n```\n\nThis ensures:\n1. **Exact row count**: Always returns exactly `n` rows as requested\n2. **No duplicate index issues**: Uses positional indexing, not label-based\n3. **Clean indices**: Resets the index to `[0, 1, 2, ..., n-1]` to avoid future issues\n\n### Changes Made\n\n1. **`xopt/generators/ga/cnsga.py`** (line 320-321): Fixed `cnsga_select` to use `.iloc` instead of `.loc`\n\n2. **`tests/generators/ga/test_cnsga.py`**: Added two comprehensive test functions:\n   - `test_cnsga_population_size_with_duplicate_indices()`: Tests that adding DataFrames with duplicate indices maintains population size\n   - `test_cnsga_chunked_evaluation()`: Tests that chunked evaluations don't cause population to exceed limit\n\n### Verification\n\nThe fix was verified with a demonstration showing:\n- OLD code with duplicate indices `[0,0,0,1,1,1]` returned 12 rows instead of 4\n- NEW code correctly returns exactly 4 rows\n- Works with all-duplicate indices (all set to same value)\n- Resets index to clean integer sequence",
